images:
  - file: Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-55-28.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-55-28.jpg/250px-Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-55-28.jpg
    width: 8179
    height: 6032
    caption: The Temple of Apollo was built in the Doric order on the ruins of
      earlier temple, being a good example of peripteral temple, supported by 38
      columns, 7 of which are still in place.
  - file: Archeological_Site_in_Ancient_Corinth.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Archeological_Site_in_Ancient_Corinth.jpg/250px-Archeological_Site_in_Ancient_Corinth.jpg
    width: 4288
    height: 2848
    caption: The archeological site located close to Temple of Apollo.
  - file: Ancient_Theater_in_Archeological_site_of_Ancient_Corinth.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Ancient_Theater_in_Archeological_site_of_Ancient_Corinth.jpg/250px-Ancient_Theater_in_Archeological_site_of_Ancient_Corinth.jpg
    width: 4288
    height: 2373
    caption: The archeological site of Ancient Theater first built in Corinth in 5th
      c. BC. The Theater could seat around 15,000 spectators.
  - file: CorintoScaviTempioApollo.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/CorintoScaviTempioApollo.jpg/250px-CorintoScaviTempioApollo.jpg
    width: 2048
    height: 1536
    caption: Temple of Apollo, Ancient Corinth
  - file: Periander_Pio-Clementino_Inv276.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Periander_Pio-Clementino_Inv276.jpg/250px-Periander_Pio-Clementino_Inv276.jpg
    width: 1632
    height: 2862
    caption: Periander (Περίανδρος, Períandros, r. 627–587 BC)
  - file: Ravel_1008.2.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Ravel_1008.2.jpg/250px-Ravel_1008.2.jpg
    width: 600
    height: 293
    caption: "Corinthian stater. Obverse: Pegasus with the archaic letter koppa (Ϙ)
      beneath the breast. Reverse: Athena wearing Corinthian helmet. Koppa
      originally stood for Ϙόρινθος (Qórinthos), the earliest spelling of the
      city's name in Doric Greek, but came to symbolize Corinth during the
      Classical period, as it had otherwise fallen out of use in favour of kappa
      (Κ) by the 5th century B.C."
  - file: Korinthos_Statues_2008-09-12.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Korinthos_Statues_2008-09-12.jpg/250px-Korinthos_Statues_2008-09-12.jpg
    width: 3791
    height: 2441
    caption: Statues in the Archaeological Museum of Ancient Corinth
  - file: GR-korinth-tempel-octavia.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/GR-korinth-tempel-octavia.jpg/250px-GR-korinth-tempel-octavia.jpg
    width: 1209
    height: 806
    caption: Corinthian order columns in ancient Corinth
  - file: CorintoScaviStrada.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/CorintoScaviStrada.jpg/250px-CorintoScaviStrada.jpg
    width: 2048
